<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Angoulême</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Angoulême is a city in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> on a hill above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charente">Charente</a>, capital of the Charente <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a>. From 866 it was the seat of counts descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vulgrin-i-count-of-angouleme">Vulgrin I</a>, later the Taillefer line and the Lusignans, and it passed to the French crown in 1308. Its domed Romanesque cathedral of Saint-Pierre dates from the 12th century, and the city now hosts a major comics festival.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/angouleme.jpg" alt="Angoulême" width="320" height="352" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Foulques1996 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Angoulememosaique.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The dukes commanded the service of their vassals, the counts and viscounts of <b>Angoulême</b>, Périgord, La Marche and Limoges and the lords of the castles that multiplied across the region from the late tenth century, but these vassals were often in revolt, and much ducal warfare was spent…</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-aquitaine">Duchy of Aquitaine · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>King John of England married Isabella of Angoulême, heiress of the county of <b>Angoulême</b>, in Angoulême Cathedral.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-24">24 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Louis XII died without a son, and his twenty-year-old cousin and son-in-law Francis of <b>Angoulême</b> succeeded him.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-1">1 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Isabella of Angoulême, heiress of the county of <b>Angoulême</b> and second wife of King John, was crowned queen of England.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-8">8 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 866 Charles the Bald appointed his kinsman Vulgrin I count of <b>Angoulême</b> and Périgord to defend the region against Viking raids.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-angouleme">County of Angoulême</a></li><li><blockquote>In 866 Charles the Bald entrusted the counties of Périgord and <b>Angoulême</b> to Vulgrin, a kinsman, to resist Viking raids along the rivers of western France.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-perigord">County of Périgord</a></li></ul></section>
